<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/category> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/category/Economic_Sciences> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureate> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/733> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/share> "2" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Economic Sciences 2000, Daniel L. McFadden" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/year> "2000"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/contribution> "Showed how to statistically handle fundamental aspects of microdata, namely data on the most important decisions we make in life: the choice of education, occupation, place of residence, marital status, number of children, so called discrete choices."@en .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Award> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/768> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/LaureateAward> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/744> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureateAward> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/category> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/category/Economic_Sciences> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureate> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/744> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/share> "3" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Economic Sciences 2001, George A. Akerlof" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/year> "2001"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/contribution> "Studied markets where sellers of products have more information than buyers about product quality. He showed that low-quality products may squeeze out high-quality products in such markets, and that prices of high-quality products may suffer as a result."@en .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Award> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/778> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/LaureateAward> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/745> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureateAward> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/category> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/category/Economic_Sciences> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureate> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/745> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/share> "3" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Economic Sciences 2001, A. Michael Spence" .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/year> "2001"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/contribution> "Showed how the able agents may improve the market outcome by taking costly action to signal information to poorly informed recipients. An important example is education as a signal of high individual productivity in the labor market. It is not necessary for education to have intrinsic value. Costly investment in education as such signals high ability.  "@en .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Award> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/779> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/LaureateAward> .
<http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureate/746> <http://data.nobelprize.org/terms/laureateAward> <http://data.nobelprize.org/resource/laureateaward/780> .
